{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is a close analog of elacridar and a fluoroelacridar tracer, sharing the 6,7-dimethoxy-tetrahydroisoquinoline head, ethylene linker, and N-phenylamide connection. Direct PET and in vivo studies show that elacridar and its fluoro analog have very low brain uptake in rats, mice, and humans and are actively transported by Pgp and BCRP/ABCG2 at the BBB. Since the query appends an additional polar dimethoxybenzamide-quinoline carboxamide tail, this transferable efflux-limited CNS exposure evidence predicts fail.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive",
      "molecule_ids": ["Molecule 1", "Molecule 3"],
      "record_ids": ["Record 1-1", "Record 3-1", "Record 3-2", "Record 3-3", "Record 3-4", "Record 3-5", "Record 3-7", "Record 3-8", "Record 3-9", "Record 3-10"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "A single mouse microPET record reports high brain uptake of [11C]elacridar, but this is inconsistent with the large body of multi-species and human PET data showing efflux-limited low brain penetration; it cannot support adequate CNS access for the larger, more polar query molecule.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory",
      "molecule_ids": ["Molecule 3"],
      "record_ids": ["Record 3-6"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Several smaller tetrahydroisoquinoline-containing radiotracers without the large amide tail, such as MC113, MC80, SA4503, MC18, and [18F]16/21, show high brain uptake. This shows that the THIQ head group alone can be brain-penetrant, but these compounds lack the elacridar-like efflux-prone amide/heteroaromatic tail and have substantially lower molecular weight and polarity, so this evidence does not transfer to the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory",
      "molecule_ids": ["Molecule 2", "Molecule 4", "Molecule 5", "Molecule 7", "Molecule 9"],
      "record_ids": ["Record 2-1", "Record 4-1", "Record 5-1", "Record 5-2", "Record 7-1", "Record 9-1"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Imatinib, despite being smaller and relatively lipophilic, has very poor CSF-to-plasma and brain-to-plasma ratios because it is a P-gp/ABCG2 substrate at the BBB. This reinforces that efflux liability can override size and lipophilicity; the query's multiple amides, aromatic rings, and quinoline motif are consistent with an efflux-substrate liability.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive",
      "molecule_ids": ["Molecule 6"],
      "record_ids": ["Record 6-1", "Record 6-2", "Record 6-3", "Record 6-4", "Record 6-5", "Record 6-6", "Record 6-7", "Record 6-8", "Record 6-9", "Record 6-10"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Additional but less structurally similar compounds, cilobradine and FJRD, also show poor BBB penetration in vivo, providing weaker supportive evidence that molecules with multiple polar/aromatic motifs and likely transporter interactions frequently fail to achieve meaningful CNS exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive",
      "molecule_ids": ["Molecule 8", "Molecule 10"],
      "record_ids": ["Record 8-1", "Record 10-1"]
    }
  ],
  "final_prediction": "fail"
}
